AB  - Macroalgae mariculture is promoted as a marine carbon dioxide removal (mCDR) strategy, particularly in East Asia. In practice, however, macroalgae is frequently co-cultured with fish and shellfish, complicating carbon budgets and potentially altering net carbon metabolism. While most work has emphasized organic carbon cycles, carbonate system responses under integrated aquaculture remain underexplored. In this study, we conducted seasonal surveys in Sansha Bay, one of the world' largest mariculture zones. Contrary to expectation, the bay persistently outgassed CO2 during winter (the seaweed growth peak season), spring, and fall. Sea surface CO2 partial pressure (pCO(2)) reached 500-1100 mu atm with air-sea CO2 fluxes of 2.1-7.0 mmol m(-2) d(-1). Against an estimated natural background of 200 mu atm (a strong sink), long-term effects of cultivation diverged by trophic group: seaweed cultivation lowered pCO(2) by 42 +/- 5 mu atm, shellfish farming increased it by 36 +/- 4 mu atm, and fish farming raised it by 375 +/- 18 mu atm, elevating mean pCO(2) to similar to 567 +/- 20 mu atm and transforming the system from a CO2 sink to a source. In this semi-enclosed bay, dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC) generated from fish farming overwhelms algal uptake, driving increases in DIC and pCO(2) and reducing the region's carbon sequestration capacity. Seasonal submarine groundwater discharge added similar to 30-60 mu atm to pCO(2), and short-term mariculture activities could episodically elevate pCO(2) up to 1100 mu atm. Analysis of the dissolved inorganic carbon stable carbon isotope (delta C-13(DIC)) indicates that seasonal increases in DIC and pCO(2) in Sansha Bay are due to the decomposition of residual seaweed biomass in late spring and organic matter respiration from fish feed in fall. To achieve mCDR and protect coastal environments, it is essential to reduce formulated feed use or develop alternative environmentally friendly fish farming methods.
