AB  - Carbon dioxide removal (CDR) is increasingly expected to complement deep emissions reductions, yet existing reviews often remain pathway-specific and provide a limited basis for comparing nature-based and engineered options within a shared analytical framework. Here, we present a systematic review and evidence synthesis of carbon sink pathways across terrestrial, ocean, mineral, and engineered systems. We develop a unified cross-pathway comparison framework spanning five dimensions-removal capacity, storage durability, measurement, reporting, and verification (MRV) tractability, resource constraints, and co-benefits/risks-and apply it to terrestrial and ocean sinks as well as bioenergy with carbon capture and storage (BECCS), direct air carbon capture and storage (DACCS), enhanced weathering, and ocean alkalinity enhancement. The synthesis identifies three recurrent bottlenecks that constrain deployability at scale: fragmented institutions and crediting rules, lack of interoperable MRV, and system stability risks coupled with resource competition. Building on these findings, we propose a Pathway-Region-Time (P-R-T) framework that translates pathway heterogeneity into regionally and temporally differentiated portfolio design, and a Carbon Flow-Storage-Feedback (CF-S-F) framework that links carbon flows, storage integrity, monitoring, and adaptive feedbacks under uncertainty. Together, these frameworks clarify how heterogeneous carbon sink pathways can be compared, sequenced, and governed across contrasting ecological and institutional settings. Rather than merely updating pathway-specific evidence, this review reframes carbon sink assessment as a systems problem of cross-pathway comparison, portfolio deployment, and adaptive governance. The results further indicate that no single pathway is universally optimal; instead, robust carbon sink strategies depend on how heterogeneous pathways are combined, matched to regional conditions, and coordinated across transition stages.
