TI  - Net primary production of cultivated Saccharina japonica (Laminariales) as an indicator of carbon dioxide removal potential in seaweed aquaculture
T2  - AQUACULTURE
LA  - English
KW  - Dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC) uptake
KW  - Marine carbon dioxide removal (mCDR)
KW  - Net primary production (NPP)
KW  - Saccharina japonica
KW  - Seaweed aquaculture
KW  - MITIGATION
KW  - FLUX
AB  - Seaweed aquaculture has been increasingly recognized as a potential nature-based solution for marine carbon dioxide removal (mCDR), yet quantitative estimates of carbon uptake remain limited, particularly at cultivation scales. In this study, we estimated the net primary production (NPP) of cultivated kelp (Saccharina japonica) by integrating in situ measurements of dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC) uptake with irradiance and biomass dynamics. Seasonal photosynthesis-irradiance (P-E) relationships were derived from field incubation experiments and combined with photosynthetically active radiation (PAR) data to model daily DIC uptake rates. These rates were then scaled with biomass variability over the cultivation cycle to estimate monthly and annual NPP. The results revealed strong seasonal variability, with low carbon uptake during early growth (January-April) followed by a rapid increase toward summer, reaching up to 42,644 and 74,947 tons C month(-1) in July and August, respectively. When scaled to the aquaculture area of South Jeolla Province (4734.45 ha), annual NPP was estimated at 1.404 kg C m(-2) yr(-1) under typical harvesting conditions, in which biomass was removed at the usual harvest period, and up to 4.168 kg C m(-2) yr(-1) under the no-harvest scenario, in which biomass was retained within the cultivation system through the annual cycle. These values exceed those reported for natural kelp populations, reflecting the enhanced productivity of intensive aquaculture systems. This study estimated the NPP of cultivated Saccharina japonica as an indicator of photosynthetic carbon assimilation, providing a foundation for future studies to assess mCDR potential by tracking the fate of fixed carbon and determining its longterm sequestration and durability.
