KW  - REDUCTION
KW  - MINERALIZATION
KW  - STORAGE
KW  - CACO3
AB  - CO2 sequestration is a critical strategy for mitigating large-scale CO2 emissions. Mineral trapping, where CO2 is converted into stable carbonate minerals, offers the most permanent storage solution, but its natural rate is typically slow. Sulfate-reducing bacteria (SRB), tolerant to anaerobic conditions, show great potential in for accelerating this process. However, the efficiency of SRB-induced carbonate precipitation is influenced by multiple, interdependent factors that remain insufficiently understood, limiting its practical application. This study systematically investigates the effects of carbon source, initial Ca2+ concentration, and pH on SRB metabolism and mineral precipitation under geochemical conditions representative of CO2 sequestration environments. Results indicate that calcium concentrations and pH conditions comparable to seawater are optimal for SRB-induced mineralization, with lactate further enhancing alkalinity generation and driving the precipitation of CaCO3 and FeS. Under these conditions, a high CaCO3 precipitation efficiency of 52.6 % and DOC consumption of 34.0 % were achieved-significantly surpassing traditional mineralization rates. Microscopic analysis indicates that elevated alkalinity promotes preferential mineral nucleation and growth, accelerating the overall process. An alkalinity-centered mechanistic model is proposed to explain the coupled interactions governing SRB-induced mineralization. These findings advance the understanding of SRB-enhanced CO2 sequestration and provide a foundation for optimizing practical applications.
