TI  - Multi-dimensional feasibility assessment of macroalgal carbon for China's carbon neutrality
T2  - ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT ASSESSMENT REVIEW
LA  - English
KW  - Carbon neutrality
KW  - Macroalgae cultivation
KW  - Resource constraints
KW  - Feasibility assessment
KW  - SERVICES
KW  - IMPACT
AB  - China's carbon neutrality strategy has intensified interest in blue carbon solutions, with macroalgal carbon increasingly considered a potential contributor to its 2060 climate goals. Here, we develop a goal-oriented quantitative framework that disaggregates carbon removal requirements, estimates nitrogen and phosphorus demands, and evaluates the economic viability of macroalgal deposition for carbon sequestration (MDCS), thereby quantifying the spatial, resource, and economic constraints on large-scale deployment. We show that: (1) the growth of macroalgal carbon sequestration has slowed since 2015, with the average annual growth rate during 2020-2023 (3.04%) approximately 1% lower than the 2003-2023 average, indicating that spatial limitation has become the dominant constraint; (2) achieving a carbon removal target of 2.5 Gt C yr(-1) through cultivated macroalgae would require 71,918.6 km(2) yr(-1) of aquaculture area, equivalent to 3.25 times China's total mariculture area in 2023. Existing mariculture-derived nitrogen and phosphorus emissions could satisfy only 0.05% and 0.12% of the required nutrient demand, respectively, highlighting a 3-4 order-of-magnitude shortfall in nutrient supply; and (3) under the accounting boundary adopted here, MDCS would incur annual economic losses of approximately US$3.18 trillion, which is 2-4 times that of conventional mitigation pathways. The cumulative losses would reach 6.7 times China's current GDP. Although seaweed aquaculture may contribute to carbon sequestration, reliance on this pathway alone is unlikely to be economically or resource sustainable, underscoring the need for integrated carbon neutrality strategies.
