KW  - MICROALGAE
KW  - STORAGE
AB  - Global warming has caused severe global consequences, including rising atmospheric and oceanic temperatures, urban pollution, and increased disease transmission. To mitigate these effects, carbon capture technologies articularly post-combustion capture have been developed using both chemical and biological approaches. This study focuses on carbon dioxide (CO2) capture from cement factory flue gases using these two methods. While biological systems such as microalgae-based capture offer ecological benefits, chemical absorption faces challenges like high energy demand and inefficient process integration. This research evaluates optimization strategies for chemical post-combustion CO2 capture using amine-based solvents. Various process enhancements are investigated, including Lean Vapor Compression (LVC), Rich Vapor Compression (RVC), Rich Solvent Recycle (RSR), and Solvent Split Flow (SSF) configurations, aiming to reduce reboiler heat duty. After identifying the optimal setup, solar energy is partially integrated to further reduce fossil fuel dependency. Three common solvents Diethanolamine (DEA), Methyldiethanolamine (MDEA), and Diglycolamine (DGA) are compared, with DGA demonstrating the lowest energy requirement (1080.4 kW versus 1850.11 kW for MDEA and 4329.18 kW for DEA). The LVC configuration improved exergy efficiency and exergoeconomic factor by 53 % and 62 %, respectively, over the base case. Solar integration led to an additional 5 % improvement in the exergoeconomic factor. For comparison, a microalgae-based biological capture system was assessed. Although more economical for flue gas flow rates below 6 kg/s, the optimized chemical method became similar to 50 $/hour more cost-effective at 10 kg/s. Thus, for large-scale cement plant emissions, the hybrid chemical solar approach proves superior.
