AB  - The rapid rise in energy demand has worsened the global climate crisis. Marine CO2 sequestration represents a promising strategy for mitigating global warming. Basalt, the most abundant rock on the seabed, naturally undergoes in situ mineralization reactions. This study investigates the combined effects of basalt mineralization and CO2 hydrates in low-temperature marine environments, focusing on dissolution-precipitation reactions and CO2 hydrate formation in the CO2-seawater-basalt system. The results indicate that under varying reaction conditions, Si, Ca, and Mg dissolution concentrations in the system follow similar trends, with alternating dissolutionprecipitation processes. The CO2 hydrate film at the interface enhances CO2 storage stability. The dissolution kinetics gradually decreased over the extended reaction period, stabilizing after 22 days. The logarithmic dissolution rate declined from an initial value of -15.11 to -17.02 mol/(cm2 & sdot;s). In low-temperature marine environments (4 degrees C and 8 degrees C), basalt effectively promotes CO2 mineralization, leading to the formation of valuable carbonate minerals, including magnesium carbonate, aragonite, and calcite. After 22 days of reaction, the extent of basalt mineralization increased by 10.39% and 22.91%, respectively. The oxygen and hydroxyl groups on the basalt surface, along with secondary minerals such as montmorillonite, enabled basalt particles to accelerate the hydrate film formation. At a 20 wt% basalt concentration, the hydrate film formation time was reduced by 73.4%. In low-temperature marine environments, basalt mineralization combined with CO2 hydrates increased CO2 uptake by 13.6 times, demonstrating a significant carbon sequestration advantage.
