AB  - Respiration and calcium carbonate (CaCO3) production by the farmed short-neck clam Ruditapes philippinarum were calculated to assess their importance as carbon dioxide (CO2) sink/source in a lagoon of the Po Delta River (Italy). Biomass and calcimass were established by monthly harvests during a 1-year period (2009). The ratio of CO2 released to CaCO3 precipitated was calculated as a function of the near-bottom temperature. From our estimates, R. philippinarum sequestered 8.18 mol(co2) m(-2) year(-1) for shell formation, but the CO2 fluxes due to respiration and calcification resulted 22.7 and 5.56 mol(co2)m(-2) year(-1), respectively. Clam farming seems therefore to be a significant additional source of CO2 to seawater. (C) 2012 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
