AB  - Inland waters are vital to the global carbon cycle, yet bacterial-driven carbon sequestration in karst aquatic systems with intense carbonate weathering and high dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC) concentrations remains unclear. This study examines a typical karst reservoir in southwestern China, combining seasonal field sampling and in situ microbial incubation experiments to investigate the bacterial-driven transformation of dissolved organic carbon (DOC) into refractory dissolved organic carbon (RDOC) in karst waters. The results show that thermal stratification creates vertically heterogeneous microenvironments, significantly influencing dissolved organic matter (DOM) composition and bacterial community structure. Bacteria-DOM coupling varies across layers, with the thermocline serving as a key zone for DOM transformation. During the strong thermal stratification (STS) period, RDOC conversion efficiency is approximately 2.6 times higher than during the isothermal mixing (IM) and early thermal stratification (ETS) periods. Overall, thermal stratification regulates microhabitats and bacterial community structure, while high DIC concentrations enhance carbon substrate supply; together, these factors promote the activity of the microbial carbon pump (MCP) in karst waters. This study reveals the potential for RDOC accumulation in karst aquatic systems and confirms their role as an important microbially driven "hidden carbon sink".
